          <compound>
            <id type="integer">1366</id>
            <name>4-Hydroxycinnamic acid</name>
            <hmdb-id>HMDB0002035</hmdb-id>
            <description>4-Hydroxycinnamic acid, also known as trans-p-hydroxycinnamate or p-coumaric acid, belongs to the class of organic compounds known as hydroxycinnamic acids. Hydroxycinnamic acids are compounds containing an cinnamic acid where the benzene ring is hydroxylated. 4-Hydroxycinnamic acid exists as a solid, slightly soluble (in water), and a weakly acidic compound (based on its pKa). 4-Hydroxycinnamic acid has been detected in multiple biofluids, such as feces, urine, and blood. Within the cell, 4-hydroxycinnamic acid is primarily located in the cytoplasm and mitochondria. 4-Hydroxycinnamic acid is also a parent compound for other transformation products, including but not limited to, methyl p-coumarate acetate, brevipolide a, and 6-O-(trans-4-coumaroyl)-beta-D-glucopyranose. Outside of the human body, 4-hydroxycinnamic acid can be found in a number of food items such as cloves, sesame, flaxseed, and tarragon. This makes 4-hydroxycinnamic acid a potential biomarker for the consumption of these food products. 4-Hydroxycinnamic acid is a yellowish green crystalline powder. Cinnamic acid and its derivatives are used as important components in flavours, perfumes, synthetic indigo and pharmaceuticals.</description>
            <cas>7400-08-0</cas>
